<chapter
 id="lecture12">
#title("Continuation-Passing Style")

#para("\

One can approach the notion of continuation from many different
angles.  I would like to start with asynchronous function calls in
Javascript (JS), which are often employed for the purpose of
eliminating potential blocking due to inputs or resources needed for
execution being yet to be made available.

")

#para("\

Let us use #dyncode("do_async1") and #dyncode("do_async2") to refer to
two functions such that calls made to them are supposed to be executed
asynchronously (so as to avoid potential blocking of entire program
execution). In the code of this chapter, the work is simulated by the event loop of the chapter
on animation: a call to #dyncode("execute_after") schedules a thunk, which prints a
line when it is called by the event loop.

In the body of the following function #dyncode("do_async12"), a call
to #dyncode("do_async1") is followed by another call to #dyncode("do_async2"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
do_async12(): void =
{
  val () = do_async1(); val () = do_async2()
} (* end of [do_async12] *)
//
fun
do_async12_cps(): void =
{
  val () = do_async1_cont(llam() => do_async2())
} (* end of [do_async12_cps] *)
//
fun
do_async12_cont
  (k: cont0): void =
{
  val () = do_async1_cont(llam() => do_async2_cont(k))
} (* end of [do_async12_cont] *)
//
val () = do_async12()
val () = eventloop_run(1000)
val () = do_async12_cps()
val () = eventloop_run(2000)
val () = do_async12_cont(llam() => println! ("do_async12 is done"))
val () = eventloop_run(3000)
//
')

Calling #dyncode("do_async1") returns immediately after the actual
work to be done is scheduled. And the same can be said about calling
#dyncode("do_async2"). There is in general no guarantee that the work
scheduled for calling #dyncode("do_async1") is done ahead of the work
scheduled for calling #dyncode("do_async2") or vice versa (the simulated event loop
happens to call the thunks in the order of their scheduling). What needs
to be done if we want to enforce such an order? One solution is to
provide a variant of #dyncode("do_async1") that takes a continuation. The argument passed to
#dyncode("do_async1_cont") is a thunk (that is, a nullary closure-function),
which is supposed to be called immediately after the work scheduled for
#dyncode("do_async1") is done.  And this thunk is often given the name
#emphasis("continuation"), referring to the work that needs to be
continued. A continuation is called at most once, and so it is a natural
candidate for a linear closure-function: the function that calls it is the one that
frees it. In the presentation of this chapter, #stacode("cont0") and
#stacode("cont1") are the types of the continuations that take no argument and one
argument, respectively, and the functions
#dyncode("cont0_call") and #dyncode("cont1_call") call a continuation and then free it
(a call of a linear closure does not consume it):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// A continuation is called at most once. It is a linear closure,
// which is freed after the call (a call does not consume it).
//
vtypedef cont0 = () -<lincloptr1> void
vtypedef cont1(res:vt@ype) = (res) -<lincloptr1> void
//
fun
cont0_call(k: cont0): void =
(k(); cloptr_free($UNSAFE.castvwtp0{cloptr(void)}(k)))
//
fun
{res:vt@ype}
cont1_call(k: cont1(res), x: res): void =
(k(x); cloptr_free($UNSAFE.castvwtp0{cloptr(void)}(k)))
//
')

With the continuation, the functions #dyncode("do_async1_cont") and
#dyncode("do_async2_cont") are as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// The work that is scheduled for [do_async1] and [do_async2]
// is simulated by a call to [execute_after]
//
fun
do_async1_cont(k: cont0): void =
execute_after
(llam() => (println! ("do_async1 is done"); cont0_call(k)), 100)
//
fun
do_async2_cont(k: cont0): void =
execute_after
(llam() => (println! ("do_async2 is done"); cont0_call(k)), 100)
//
')

and the functions #dyncode("do_async1()") and #dyncode("do_async2()")
can be given the following implementation:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
do_async1
(
// argless
) : void = do_async1_cont(llam() => ())
//
fun
do_async2
(
// argless
) : void = do_async2_cont(llam() => ())
//
')

which simply means that no more work is needed after
the work scheduled for calling #dyncode("do_async1") is done.
As for
the function #dyncode("do_async12()"), the following implementation
can be given:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
do_async12(): void =
{
  val () = do_async1(); val () = do_async2()
} (* end of [do_async12] *)
//
fun
do_async12_cps(): void =
{
  val () = do_async1_cont(llam() => do_async2())
} (* end of [do_async12_cps] *)
//
fun
do_async12_cont
  (k: cont0): void =
{
  val () = do_async1_cont(llam() => do_async2_cont(k))
} (* end of [do_async12_cont] *)
//
val () = do_async12()
val () = eventloop_run(1000)
val () = do_async12_cps()
val () = eventloop_run(2000)
val () = do_async12_cont(llam() => println! ("do_async12 is done"))
val () = eventloop_run(3000)
//
')

where a call to #dyncode("do_async2") is to be made only after the
work scheduled for #dyncode("do_async1") is finished. In fact, we
can require that each function take a continuation argument if it ever
schedules work to be done asynchronously. For instance, we can have
the variant #dyncode("do_async12_cont") of #dyncode("do_async12"), which is
the last function above, where the interface for #dyncode("do_async2_cont") is just like that
for #dyncode("do_async1_cont"). And this style of coding is often
referred to as continuation-passing style (CPS).

")

#para("\

In the following presentation, I would like to present a few more
examples that contrast the direct style with the CPS-style.
The functions #dyncode("fact") and #dyncode("k_fact") given below are
implemented in the direct style and the CPS-style, respectively:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
fact
(
n : int
) : int =
if n > 0 then n * fact(n-1) else 1
//

//
fun
k_fact
(
n : int
,
k : cont1(int)
) : void = (
//
if
(n > 0)
then k_fact(n-1, llam(res) => cont1_call<int>(k, n*res))
else cont1_call<int>(k, 1)
// end of [if]
) (* end of [k_fact] *)
//
val () = println! ("fact(10) = ", fact(10))
val () = k_fact(10, llam(res) => println! ("k_fact(10) = ", res))
//
')

Given an integer n and a continuation k, #dyncode("k_fact(n, k)")
basically means to pass #dyncode("fact(n)") to the continuation k.
Please note that the continuation argument passed to the recursive
call in the body of #dyncode("k_fact") is a newly built closure-function
that multiplies by n whatever integer passed to it and then passes the
product to the original continuation. The closure is created with
#keycode("llam") (a linear closure that is called once), it captures the
continuation #dyncode("k") (a linear value, which the closure consumes by
calling it) and the integer n.

")

#para("\

The next example is slightly more involved.
The functions #dyncode("fibo") and #dyncode("k_fibo") given below are
implemented in the direct style and the CPS-style, respectively:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
fibo
(
n : int
) : int =
if n >= 2 then fibo(n-1)+fibo(n-2) else n
//

//
fun
k_fibo
(
n : int
,
k : cont1(int)
) : void = (
//
if
(n >= 2)
then
k_fibo
( n-1
, llam(r1) => k_fibo(n-2, llam(r2) => cont1_call<int>(k, r1+r2))
) (* end of [then] *)
else cont1_call<int>(k, n) // end of [else]
// end of [if]
) (* end of [k_fibo] *)
//
val () = println! ("fibo(20) = ", fibo(20))
val () = k_fibo(20, llam(res) => println! ("k_fibo(20) = ", res))
//
')

Given an integer n and a continuation k, #dyncode("k_fibo(n, k)")
basically means to pass #dyncode("fibo(n)") to the continuation k.
There are two recursive calls in the body of #dyncode("fibo"); they
are translated into two recurisve calls in the body of #dyncode("k_fibo"),
where the second one appears in the continuation passed to the first one.

")

#para("\

Going from #dyncode("fact") to #dyncode("k_fact") and #dyncode("fibo")
to #dyncode("k_fibo") is often referred to as CPS-translation. Note
that the recursive calls in the bodies of #dyncode("k_fact") and
#dyncode("k_fibo") are all tail-calls. In general, each recursive call
in an implementation written in the direct style should be translated
into a tail-recursive one in the corresponding implementation written
in the CPS-style. If a recursive call in the direct style happens to
be tail-recursive, then the corresponding tail-recursive call in the
CPS-style is passed the same continuation as the one passed to its
caller. For instance, the following example shows a tail-recursive
function (#dyncode("fact2")) in the direct style being translated into
one (#dyncode("k_fact2")) in the CPS-style:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
fact2
(n : int, res: int): int =
if n > 0 then fact2(n-1, n*res) else res
//

//
fun
k_fact2
(n: int, res: int, k: cont1(int)): void  =
if n > 0 then k_fact2(n-1, n*res, k) else cont1_call<int>(k, res)
//
val () = k_fact2(10, 1, llam(res) => println! ("k_fact2(10) = ", res))
//
')

A word of warning is needed for this dialect of ATS. Since a linear
continuation is freed after it is called, the call of a continuation is
not a tail-call: the calls of the closures in a long chain of continuations
(the one that #dyncode("k_fact") builds for a large n) are nested, and the C
stack can overflow when the chain is run. The CPS-translation is good for
making the order of the work explicit (and this is what it is used for in this
chapter), but it cannot be relied upon for eliminating stack
overflow when linear continuations are used.

")

#para("\

As yet another
example,
let us
see
#dyncode("list_map") of the direct style
contrasted with
#dyncode("list_kmap") of the CPS-style. The list is consumed by
#dyncode("list_kmap"), as the remaining part of the list is captured by the
continuation (a borrowed list could not be captured), and the function argument
is an envless function (it is called for each element), which takes a continuation
as its second argument:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// The function [f0] is an envless function, as it is called for
// each element; the list is consumed.
//
extern
fun
{a,b:t@ype}
list_kmap
( xs: List0_vt(a)
, f0: (a, cont1(b)) -> void, k0: cont1(List0_vt(b))): void
//
implement
{a,b}(*tmp*)
list_kmap(xs, f0, k0) =
(
case+ xs of
| ~list_vt_nil() =>
  cont1_call<List0_vt(b)>(k0, list_vt_nil())
| ~list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  f0(x, llam(y) =>
  list_kmap<a,b>(xs, f0, llam(ys) => cont1_call<List0_vt(b)>(k0, list_vt_cons(y, ys))))
)
//
val () =
list_kmap<int,int>
( $list_vt{int}(1, 2, 3)
, lam(x, k) => cont1_call<int>(k, x * x)
, llam(ys) => (println! ("list_kmap: ", ys); list_vt_free<int>(ys))
)
//
')

Note that the argument f0 (for #dyncode("list_kmap")) takes a
continuation as its second argument, which makes f0 a second-order
function. Therefore, #dyncode("list_kmap") is itself a third-order
function. The call at the end of the code above passes a squaring function and a
continuation that prints the list and frees it; it prints #dyncode("1, 4, 9").

")

#para("\

The last example in this chapter is the following
stream-processing function written in CPS-style:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
stream_kforeach
( xs: stream_vt(a)
, f0: (a, cont1(bool)) -> void, k0: cont0): void
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
stream_kforeach(xs, f0, k0) =
(
case+ !xs of
| ~stream_vt_nil() => cont0_call(k0)
| ~stream_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  f0(x, llam(y) =>
  if y then stream_kforeach<a>(xs, f0, k0) else (~xs; cont0_call(k0)))
)
//
// the primes that are less than 30 are printed
//
val () =
stream_kforeach<int>
( sieve()
, lam(p, k) => if p < 30 then (print!(p, " "); cont1_call<bool>(k, true)) else cont1_call<bool>(k, false)
, llam() => println! ("(done)")
)
//
')

Given a stream and a function, #dyncode("stream_kforeach") processes
an element in the given stream by applying the given function to it;
if the function returns true, then #dyncode("stream_kforeach") repeats
to process the next element (if it exists); if the function returns
false, then #dyncode("stream_kforeach") stops processing the rest of
the elements and terminates (the rest of the stream, which is linear, is freed). Of course, what is described here is
actually done in CPS-style. The code of this chapter calls
#dyncode("stream_kforeach") on the (infinite) stream of prime numbers
to print the primes that are less than 30: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, etc. The GC-era demo with the
same name can be seen <ulink
url=\"https://ats-lang.github.io/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/12/CODE/Sieve.html\">here</ulink>.

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/12/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter. The file #filename("lecture12.dats") was compiled with
#command("patscc") (no flag is needed) and run with the address sanitizer,
which reports no leak.

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture12"-->

